=============================================================
  Joint Hypothesis Tests (Wald)
=============================================================

Error-correction term as a whole
  H0: alpha = 0
  Wald chi2(3) = 16.2711, p-value = 0.0010
  ! NOT A TEST: under H0, beta is unidentified (Davies).  Use -lrtest -bootstrap

Weak exogeneity, one variable at a time   [H0: row i of alpha = 0, chi2(1)]

BUFFALO (nabla Y2 block):
  H0: this variable does not adjust
  Wald chi2(1) = 13.0979, p-value = 0.0003
  REJECT H0 -> BUFFALO adjusts to the disequilibrium.

MINNEAPOLIS (nabla Y2 block):
  H0: this variable does not adjust
  Wald chi2(1) = 15.6846, p-value = 0.0001
  REJECT H0 -> MINNEAPOLIS adjusts to the disequilibrium.

KANSASCITY (Y1 block):
  H0: this variable does not adjust
  Wald chi2(1) = 9.1056, p-value = 0.0025
  REJECT H0 -> KANSASCITY adjusts to the disequilibrium.

Exclusion from the cointegrating relations   [H0: row i of beta_2 = 0, chi2(1)]
  only the 2 nabla Y2 variables can be excluded: beta = [I_r ; beta_2] normalises on Y1

BUFFALO:
  H0: row 1 of beta_2 = 0
  Wald chi2(1) = 1.0163, p-value = 0.3134
  Cannot reject H0 -> BUFFALO can be dropped from it.

MINNEAPOLIS:
  H0: row 2 of beta_2 = 0
  Wald chi2(1) = 9.7076, p-value = 0.0018
  REJECT H0 -> MINNEAPOLIS belongs in the long-run relation.

All of B2 jointly:
  H0: B2 = 0 (the relation involves only the Y1 block)
  Wald chi2(2) = 181.6814, p-value = 0.0000
  REJECT H0 -> the nabla Y2 block belongs in it.

Short-run dynamics   [Gamma on nabla Y, Theta on the innovations]
  ! statements about nabla Y, not about the levels: a variable can drive
    another's DIFFERENCES and still be tied to it only through ec

Joint significance of the last short-run lag, Gamma(1):
  H0: Gamma(last) = 0
  Wald chi2(9) = 49.0884, p-value = 0.0000
  REJECT H0 -> the last AR lag is significant.

All cross effects jointly:
  H0: every off-diagonal coefficient of Gamma and Theta = 0
  Wald chi2(6) = 40.6326, p-value = 0.0000
  REJECT H0 -> the cross structure earns its parameters.

D.BUFFALO: what the others do to it:
  H0: no other variable enters the equation of D.BUFFALO
  Wald chi2(2) = 19.1546, p-value = 0.0001
  REJECT H0 -> D.BUFFALO is driven by the others.

D.BUFFALO: what it does to the others:
  H0: D.BUFFALO enters no other equation
  Wald chi2(2) = 9.5914, p-value = 0.0083
  REJECT H0 -> D.BUFFALO drives the others.

D.MINNEAPOLIS: what the others do to it:
  H0: no other variable enters the equation of D.MINNEAPOLIS
  Wald chi2(2) = 8.3420, p-value = 0.0154
  REJECT H0 -> D.MINNEAPOLIS is driven by the others.

D.MINNEAPOLIS: what it does to the others:
  H0: D.MINNEAPOLIS enters no other equation
  Wald chi2(2) = 26.0843, p-value = 0.0000
  REJECT H0 -> D.MINNEAPOLIS drives the others.

D.KANSASCITY: what the others do to it:
  H0: no other variable enters the equation of D.KANSASCITY
  Wald chi2(2) = 7.3559, p-value = 0.0253
  REJECT H0 -> D.KANSASCITY is driven by the others.

D.KANSASCITY: what it does to the others:
  H0: D.KANSASCITY enters no other equation
  Wald chi2(2) = 2.8602, p-value = 0.2393
  Cannot reject H0 -> D.KANSASCITY does not drive the others.

  ! p-values from the BFGS-accumulated covariance; use -fdhess before quoting them


=============================================================
  Impulse Response of the Levels
=============================================================

  Response of Y_{t+k} to a one-s.d. orthogonalized shock at t.
  Shocks are orthogonalized by Sigma = P D P', so the ORDERING is the
  .inp's column order: a different order gives different shocks.
  ! in a cointegrated system the response does not die out: what it
    converges to is the permanent effect, of rank M - r = 2.

